Source package: gtk4

The four patches in series together let a GTK 4 application name its windows to the compositor through the session
management protocol, so that mutter places them again where they were. They are for Text Editor, which uses them
with patches/gnome-text-editor/. No other installed GTK 4 application opts in, and GTK changes nothing for an
application which does not set GtkApplication:support-save.

GTK keeps the window state of such an application in $XDG_STATE_HOME/<application ID>@fallback.state, for Text
Editor ~/.local/state/org.gnome.TextEditor@fallback.state. It holds the name of the application's session with
mutter and, for each window, the name mutter knows the window by and the application's own state of it. GTK
reads it when the application starts and writes it every 15 seconds while one of its windows has the focus, and
when the application quits. Moving the file away makes the application's windows open unplaced
once, and placed again from the start after that. Mutter keeps the places it stored under the old names in
~/.local/share/gnome-shell/session.gvdb, see patches/mutter/README, and so it does for a window GTK restores and
the application then destroys without showing it, as Text Editor does when it restores no documents, since GTK
cannot remove a name stored in an earlier run (mutter issue 5174).

application-save-restore-api.patch
    Upstream's revert, commit 15ed5c2fea, of the removal of the public save/restore API of GtkApplication before
    4.22 (merge request 9395), cherry-picked unchanged. It brings back GtkApplication:support-save,
    GtkApplication::restore-window, GtkApplicationWindow::save-state and the rest of the API GTK 4.21 had, as it
    was on main until upstream hid it again for 4.24 (merge request 10196), because the asynchronous API meant to
    replace it did not land in time. The work on that API is on the branch avovk/async-state-saving of GTK.

wayland-session-management-without-debug-flag.patch
    GDK 4.22 binds the protocol only with GDK_DEBUG=session-mgmt. This is upstream's commit 15da56ccc5, which
    drops that debug flag, applied by hand, since upstream made it after moving GDK from the draft protocol,
    xx_session_manager_v1, to the final one, xdg_session_manager_v1. GTK 4.22 speaks only the draft, which mutter
    offers through patches/mutter/xx-session-management.patch.

application-restore-memory-leak.patch
    Upstream's commit ebd8e0433a, which frees the list of discarded instance IDs when registering with the session
    manager.

application-register-restore-only-when-supported.patch
    GTK registers an application with gnome-session through RegisterRestore without reading gnome-session's
    RestoreSupported property. gnome-session 50 reports it as false, but its save/restore is still live: it records
    every application which registers, launches the ones still running at the end of the session again at the
    next login, and then starts none of the autostart applications of that login (do_phase_startup in its
    gsm-manager.c). With this patch GTK reads RestoreSupported first and registers only when it is true.
    Otherwise it keeps the state under its fallback instance ID, as it does without a session manager, and
    restores it at every start of the application with the restore reason launch. Mutter ignores the reason, so
    the windows are placed again either way. Neither GTK main nor the branch with the asynchronous API reads the
    property, which makes this a candidate for an upstream merge request.

application-save-restore-symbols.patch (debian-series)
    Lists gtk_application_save, gtk_application_forget and gtk_restore_reason_get_type in
    debian/libgtk-4-1.symbols.in. dh_makeshlibs runs with -c4, which fails the build on any exported symbol the
    file does not list. It changes the packaging, so it is applied from debian-series rather than series.

Ubuntu's own debian/patches already carry upstream's commit 221cd8e190, which keeps GTK from crashing when an
application forgets a window it never showed, which Text Editor does with a restored window it has no documents
for.

None of the patches adds a test to GTK's test suite, which runs during the build and shows that they break nothing
existing. patches/gnome-text-editor/tests/run-text-editor-session.sh tests with Text Editor that windows are named
and restored, see the README there.

Drop the patches once Ubuntu ships a GTK whose GtkApplication has a public save/restore API again, which will not
be this one: Text Editor then has to be ported to that API, and the RestoreSupported check checked against it. A
rebuild of GTK means a rebuild for every Ubuntu update of it. After a version bump, check that
gtk/gtkapplication-dbus.c still registers with RegisterRestore and that gdk/wayland/gdkdisplay-wayland.c still
has no session management entry in init_skip_protocols.

Check by hand after an upgrade:

  nm -D /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgtk-4.so.1 | grep -E 'gtk_application_(save|forget)'    should list both
